  "rules": {
    "connector_history_and_optional_functions_v1": "USB was introduced to standardize peripheral connections and later expanded across computers, mobile devices, power supplies, displays, and other electronics. Standard, Mini, and Micro connector forms preceded Type-C. The USB page says Type-C is reversible, is not exclusive to USB, and can support multiple protocols and functions, many of which are optional according to the hardware. It also says backward compatibility commonly reduces signaling rate, maximum offered power, or other capabilities. Therefore a shared USB-C shape does not identify the implemented data mode, power mode, alternate mode, or device role.",
    "seller_port_and_cable_claims_v1": "The ULTRANET snapshot advertises one USB-C and one USB-A port, PD and PPS, up to 65 W on the USB-C port with one device, shared allocation with two devices, and an E-Mark cable condition for its MacBook claim; these are seller statements. The ALOGIC snapshot advertises four ports and materially different port ceilings: USB-C 1 up to 100 W alone, USB-C 2 up to 18 W, the two USB-A ports sharing 17 W, and a derated 65 W plus 18 W plus 17 W allocation when all outputs are active; these are seller statements. One USB-C cable snapshot advertises 100 W, 20 Gbit/s, an E-Marker, and video only when the connected port supports DisplayPort Alternate Mode. The Anker USB-C cable snapshot advertises 60 W and lists USB 2.0 or 480 Mbit/s data behavior. These snapshots show that matching connector shapes can carry different claimed power, data, video, and allocation capabilities, but they do not independently verify performance or reliability.",
    "negotiated_path_v1": "The USB communications source says connected devices identify themselves and negotiate parameters such as speed and power through protocols including USB Power Delivery. The hardware source says current USB Power Delivery versions reach up to 240 W, while a standard USB-C cable is specified for 60 W and at least USB 2.0 data capability; it also distinguishes cables that provide power without data in the broader USB cable family. A defensible charging assessment must therefore inspect the intersection of the phone's supported profile, the selected charger port and its current sharing state, the cable's current and power capability, and the relevant protocol. Advertised maximum wattage is a ceiling under stated conditions, not a guarantee that every phone and cable combination receives it. Data rate, video support, and charging power are separate capability questions.",
    "phone_specific_profile_examples_v1": "The Galaxy S22 page states USB PD charging profiles only for that family: S22 at 25 W with PPS and 15 W without PPS, and S22+ or S22 Ultra at 45 W with PPS and 15 W without PPS. The Oppo page states that the 240 W SuperVOOC implementation in the Realme GT Neo5 requires its bundled proprietary charger and proprietary USB-C cable to use the advertised rate. These are exact examples of phone-side and bundle-side constraints. They do not establish profiles for unlisted Galaxy, Oppo, Realme, or OnePlus models and do not imply that every nominally compatible charger reaches the fastest advertised mode.",
    "community_observation_scope_v1": "One iPhone 14 Pro Max author reports slow charging near 75 percent using both MagSafe and a Lightning cable connected to a MacBook Air 30 W USB-C brick, then reports a faster increase when starting at 49 percent. This is one uncontrolled observation that changes battery state and charging method; the phone-side wired connector is Lightning, not USB-C. A separate iPhone XS Max author reports that several MFi cables stopped working after a back-glass repair while an official 20 W combination and wireless charging worked, then says compressed-air cleaning of small port debris fixed the issue. Repair timing and debris are strong confounders, so the report cannot identify a protocol or charger cause. A third author reports rapid failures of Lightning connector pins while another participant reports no official Apple cable failure. These are individual Lightning experiences and cannot establish USB-C contact life, cable reliability, or a population brand ranking.",
    "compatibility_test_matrix_v1": "For each family phone, record the exact model, phone connector, supported charging protocols and profiles from model-specific documentation, and any proprietary bundle condition. For each charger, record the exact model, selected port, that port's voltage-current profiles, PPS support, and the allocation when other ports are occupied. For each cable, record connector ends, rated current or power, USB data generation, e-marker status when relevant, and video or alternate-mode limits. Test one controlled combination at a time with the same starting battery range, temperature, phone activity, cable orientation if relevant, and elapsed interval. Log negotiated mode when observable, battery percentage change, measured power when suitable equipment is available, temperature, disconnects, and whether adding a second device changes the port allocation. A single successful test supports only that recorded combination and condition.",
    "evidence_bounded_choice_v1": "The admissible answer should explain the historical convergence on USB-C while showing why the connector does not collapse the protocol, port, phone, cable, and state layers. It should compare seller claims without treating them as independent tests, preserve every community confound and connector type, and use the S22 and Realme GT Neo5 examples only at exact scope. Because the material lacks a controlled cross-device matrix and longitudinal reliability evidence, it cannot name one universally trusted brick or cable or promise the fastest rate for every combination. It may create a conditional shortlist from explicitly documented profiles and cable capabilities, then require the per-phone matrix and controlled checks before consolidating the family's drawer.",
    "layered_usbc_decision_v1": "The final decision is to treat USB-C as a shared connector shape rather than a universal performance label. Keep only charger, port, and cable combinations whose documented protocol and capability intersect with each exact phone, then verify them in the phone-by-brick-by-port-by-cable matrix under controlled conditions. The two charger pages and two cable pages remain seller claims, the S22 and Realme GT Neo5 examples stay model-specific, and the iPhone observations remain Lightning or MagSafe troubleshooting evidence with charge-state, repair, and debris confounders. Do not call any item universally fastest or trusted because the corpus contains neither a controlled family-device matrix nor longitudinal reliability evidence."
